---
/**
 * The capability sheet.
 *
 * Keyed by file extension wherever a row is about a file type, because that is
 * how a Unity project is actually organised — the key column carries
 * information rather than decorating the row, and it continues the argument the
 * hero panel makes. The last three rows are about the app rather than a file
 * type, so they are keyed by a word instead.
 *
 * Every claim here is meant to be traceable to something in the repo. Three
 * that used to sit on this page were not: Git LFS setup, Plastic SCM
 * integration, and animator/blend-tree parsing have no implementation, so they
 * are gone rather than reworded.
 */
const rows = [
  {
    key: [".cs"],
    title: "C# that knows it is Unity",
    body:
      "Completions, go-to-definition and rename through a C# language server, plus analyzers written for this engine: allocations in Update, GetComponent in hot paths, legacy input APIs in a project already on the Input System, fields that will silently fail to serialize. Lifecycle methods are marked in the gutter, and a debugger with call stack, variables and watches attaches to the Editor.",
  },
  {
    key: [".unity", ".prefab", ".mat"],
    title: "Scenes and prefabs as structure",
    body:
      "Serialized assets open as a navigable tree instead of a few thousand lines of YAML, and changes diff as GameObjects and components rather than as text. GUIDs resolve to asset paths in both directions, so a script reference can be traced across every scene and prefab that uses it.",
  },
  {
    key: [".asset"],
    title: "ScriptableObjects as typed forms",
    body:
      "An asset resolves its script GUID, finds the class behind it and renders the real fields — the Inspector, in your editor. When a class and the instances saved against it drift apart, the agent can reconcile them.",
  },
  {
    key: [".uxml", ".uss"],
    title: "UI Toolkit with a live canvas",
    body:
      "Markup renders on a preview canvas beside its source, with an inspector for the element under the cursor. A design chat docks over the canvas so the AI can build the screen with you, and every write is validated before it lands: invalid UXML or USS is refused, not warned about.",
  },
  {
    key: [".inputactions"],
    title: "Input actions, not JSON",
    body:
      "Action maps, bindings and control schemes open as a table you can read. The agent sees your real action names, so it writes a handler for Jump instead of inventing one.",
  },
  {
    key: [".asmdef", ".shader", ".hlsl"],
    title: "And the rest of the project",
    body:
      "Assembly definitions, ShaderLab, HLSL and compute shaders are first-class too, package-cache sources open read-only, and .meta files stay paired with the assets they belong to.",
  },
  {
    key: ["Unity"],
    title: "A live link to the running Unity",
    body:
      "Play, pause and stop from the toolbar. Console logs stream in with click-to-source stack traces, tests run from the IDE and report their results back, and compile status arrives the moment scripts rebuild — including while Unity is sitting in the background.",
  },
  {
    key: ["AI"],
    title: "An agent with Unity in its hands",
    body:
      "More than thirty tools, most of them Unity-specific: read the scene hierarchy, set a property with Undo intact, run the tests, search the Unity API, scaffold a UI screen. Edits arrive as reviewable diffs behind checkpoints you can roll back, and after each turn it reads the Unity console and repairs what it broke. Bring Claude Code or another external agent instead if you prefer.",
  },
  {
    key: ["IDE"],
    title: "The IDE around all of it",
    body:
      "An integrated terminal, Git with worktrees and UnityYAMLMerge wired up for scene merges, project-wide search, a command palette, six themes, and inline completions as you type.",
  },
];
---

<section id="features" class="relative py-16 sm:py-28">
  <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
    <div class="mb-12 max-w-2xl scroll-reveal sm:mb-16">
      <h2 class="mb-4 font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl">
        Unity-native, <span class="text-gradient-orange">all the way down</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="max-w-[58ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground sm:text-lg">
        Not a general-purpose editor with a Unity plugin bolted to the side.
        Every file type the engine writes has a real editor here, and the AI
        reads all of them.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* A spec sheet, not a card grid: the key column is the file type, the
        hairline is the separator, and nothing hovers or lifts. */}
    <div class="max-w-5xl">
      {rows.map((r) => (
        <div class="grid gap-x-10 gap-y-3 border-t border-border/40 py-7 sm:grid-cols-[150px_1fr] sm:py-8">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-1 pt-0.5">
            {r.key.map((k) => (
              <span class="font-mono text-xs text-primary/80">{k}</span>
            ))}
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="mb-2 font-display text-lg font-semibold sm:text-xl">{r.title}</h3>
            <p class="max-w-[64ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">{r.body}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
      <div class="border-t border-border/40"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 scroll-reveal">
      <a
        href="/features"
        class="group inline-flex items-center gap-2 font-semibold text-primary transition-colors hover:text-primary/80"
      >
        Every feature in detail
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-5 w-5 transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1"><path d="M5 12h14"/><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"/></svg>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
